{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the Ayurvedic approach to lumbar spondylosis treatment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Ayurveda addresses the imbalance and tissue depletion contributing to degenerative change in the lower spine rather than the pain alone. At KSAC Hospitals the approach begins with a precise diagnosis, after which the treating doctor prepares a customised plan."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will I be given the same treatment as other lumbar spondylosis patients?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The plan depends on which levels are affected, how advanced the degeneration is, whether a nerve is involved and what the patient's occupation and posture demand of their back."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need to bring my X-ray or MRI to the consultation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, where available. X-rays, MRI or CT reports, along with current prescriptions and records of earlier treatment, help the doctor arrive at a precise diagnosis and plan accordingly."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is lumbar spondylosis the same as a slipped disc?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Lumbar spondylosis refers to age-related degenerative change across the discs, vertebrae and spinal joints, while a disc herniation involves disc material pushing beyond its normal boundary. The two may coexist."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can Ayurvedic treatment manage lumbar spondylosis long term?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Ongoing management is an important part of care for a degenerative condition. KSAC Hospitals supports long-term management through periodic review, prescribed medicines where indicated, mobility practices and lifestyle guidance."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When should I seek urgent assessment rather than planned treatment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Progressive weakness in the legs, numbness around the inner thighs, groin or saddle area or any change in bladder or bowel control needs immediate medical assessment. These can indicate cauda equina compression."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What lifestyle changes support lumbar spondylosis management?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Regular spinal mobility work, correct lifting and bending technique, avoiding long unbroken periods of sitting, maintaining a healthy body weight and using seating that supports the natural curve of the lower back all help."}}]}
</script>
